@* How it looks is driven by Value, through the "on" class, rather than by the checkbox's own
   :checked state.

   A checkbox is an uncontrolled DOM element: the browser flips it the moment it is clicked. If the
   handler then leaves Value as it was -- the change was refused, the save failed, the member holds
   an authority that blocks it -- Blazor re-renders, sees checked="@Value" is the same as last
   time, and leaves the DOM alone. The browser's flip stays on screen and the switch now disagrees
   with the data behind it. That is what showed as a user whose switch read off beside a status
   reading Active.

   Driving the paint from Value instead makes the two impossible to separate: the class is
   recomputed on every render, so a refused change snaps straight back. *@
<label class="cg-toggle @(Value ? "on" : "") @(Disabled ? "cg-toggle-disabled" : "")" title="@(Value ? "On" : "Off")">
    <input type="checkbox" checked="@Value" disabled="@Disabled" @onchange="OnChangeAsync" />
    <span class="cg-toggle-track"><span class="cg-toggle-thumb"></span></span>
</label>

@code {
    [Parameter] public bool Value { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<bool> ValueChanged { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    private Task OnChangeAsync(ChangeEventArgs e) => ValueChanged.InvokeAsync((bool)e.Value!);
}
